Based on the provided test code, specifically the `test_stream_flash_buffered_write_unaligned` test case, it is clear that the post-write callback is expected to be called with the number of bytes of user data being written, not including any padding bytes added for flash write alignment.

The current implementation of `flash_sync` in `subsys/storage/stream/stream_flash.c` rounds up the length to the flash write block size and passes this aligned length to the callback. This causes the test to fail when an unaligned write is flushed.

The fix is to pass the original number of bytes in the buffer (`ctx->buf_bytes`) to the callback, while still using the aligned length for the actual flash write operation.

Here is the required patch:

subsys/storage/stream/stream_flash.c
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
	if (ctx->callback) {
		rc = ctx->callback(ctx->buf, len, ctx->offset +
				   ctx->bytes_written);
		if (rc < 0) {
			return rc;
		}
	}
========
	if (ctx->callback) {
		rc = ctx->callback(ctx->buf, ctx->buf_bytes, ctx->offset +
				   ctx->bytes_written);
		if (rc < 0) {
			return rc;
		}
	}
